<p>Image from here: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pretty_scarab_beetle_(21261230285).jpg">https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pretty_scarab_beetle_(21261230285).jpg</a><br />
Licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 2.0 (CC BY-SA 2.0)</p>
<p>Big thanks to people (and pets) who contributed dialogue material for the logs:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tuomey: (housemate’s) cat</li>
<li>kaktus: two cats</li>
<li>subletea: cockatiel</li>
<li>(Zyn has two fish that she taught to make kissy faces thank you Renfish thank you Huxfish)</li>
</ul>
<p>And of course, many thanks to the reviewers in chat!</p>
<ul>
<li>Gaffsey for wording help and flow and specific details!</li>
<li>Lazar for narrative development!</li>
<li>Flawed for beetle discussion!</li>
<li>WrongJohnSilver for overall commentary!</li>
<li>Synth_Synapses for reading!</li>
<li>Vacuum for nits and picks!</li>
<li>Sirens for reading and liking the image caption!</li>
<li>Photosynthetic for talk of resins!</li>
<li>ghostpage for random details!</li>
</ul>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;to&nbsp;show&nbsp;background&nbsp;and&nbsp;rambling&nbsp;and&nbsp;stuff</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">PUT&nbsp;A&nbsp;SMALL&nbsp;BEETLE&nbsp;IN&nbsp;YOUR&nbsp;EAR</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><sub>PUT A SCARAB BEETLE RIGHT INTO YOUR FAVORITE EAR</sub></p>
<p>I read somewhere that if someone was too poor to afford a Book of the Dead in Ancient Egypt or couldn’t read, a priest could help them out by whispering spells from the Book to a scarab, killing and mummifying the scarab, and sticking that scarab in the dead person’s ear, so the ghost scarab could whisper the spells and answers to riddles to them and help them cross the underworld to get to the afterlife.</p>
<p>This person, uh… ran out of scarabs. So they use pets, because pets can identify with humans and sometimes help humans. But the pets don’t always consider magical spells to be the most important thing they’ve heard.</p>
<p>Why can the priest understand all languages but not talk? Probably got some beetles in their ears. Think Babel fish, kind of. But the beetles can only repeat things that they’ve been told.</p>
<p>All these things I looked up while writing!</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarab_(artifact">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarab_(artifact</a>)</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarabaeus_sacer">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scarabaeus_sacer</a></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Book_of_the_Dead_spells">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Book_of_the_Dead_spells</a></li>
<li><a href="http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2004/09/0915_040915_petmummies_2.html">http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2004/09/0915_040915_petmummies_2.html</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.wired.com/2013/11/egyptian-mummies-afterlife-diet/">https://www.wired.com/2013/11/egyptian-mummies-afterlife-diet/</a></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_Ra">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_Ra</a></li>
<li><a href="https://www.reference.com/history/did-ancient-egyptian-priests-wear-48f7bfe894fd9da1">https://www.reference.com/history/did-ancient-egyptian-priests-wear-48f7bfe894fd9da1</a></li>
</ul>
</div>
