					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606595966" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1497419519 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Jun 2017 05:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2851909">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I very much had a brief &quot;why are you describing the humanoid's pupils and clothing -_-&quot; moment that… I mostly stand by.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The scarlet pupils are a nod to the Egyptian deity Ra (who has a morning manifestation associated with him, which is Khepri, the scarab beetle who was believed to roll the sun up in the mornings like scarab beetles roll balls of dung), and a sort of tongue-in-cheek Easter egg regarding this: <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_Ra">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_of_Ra</a></p>
<p>The simple clothing is to reference the fact that at some point in 3223's (original?) life, he was a priest. And he would have actually put mummified scarab beetles into the ears of the deceased who couldn't read the Book of the Dead.</p>
<p>eta: the fact that the clothing can't be removed is an indication that he's kind of stuck as a priest now. Maybe he's gotta mummify a certain number of pets or produce the perfect scarab guide to the afterlife before he can move on. You decide!</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I love the book of the dead getting conflicted with all the dumb things we say to our pets. +1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I always wondered, &quot;why were scarab beetles the only animals who could guide the dead?&quot;. It was probably the mythological significance and the fact that beetles could fit in human ears, but I like to think that maybe people's beloved pets were more interested in remembering their owners' words than the Book of the Dead, so scarabs were picked for the job.</p>

							</div>

								
